00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:07.620
Society's have valued
productivity since ancient times.

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:10.950
Philosophers like Aristotle
and Plato spoke admirably about

00:07:10.950 --> 00:07:13.380
knowledge-driven achievements
and skilled craftmanship.

00:07:13.380 --> 00:07:14.460
But at the same time,

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:17.430
they cautioned that zest
for work should be balanced

00:07:17.430 --> 00:07:20.160
with a full throated
embrace of leisure time.

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:22.800
And notably, their mode
work sitting around thinking

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:24.930
for hours on end would
probably be viewed today

00:07:24.930 --> 00:07:26.850
as unproductive or naval gazey.

00:07:26.850 --> 00:07:28.380
But we don't think so.

00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:31.080
Philosophy is hard work,
and someone's got to do it.

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:34.080
But as is the case with
just about everything,

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:37.560
Christianity changed the way
we viewed work when it reframed

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:41.250
labor as a punishment cast
upon the apple curious

00:07:41.250 --> 00:07:42.720
by a wrathful God.

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:45.337
As a philosopher, Camillo Berneri writes,

00:07:45.337 --> 00:07:48.300
"Ancient and medieval
Catholicism glorified work

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:50.490
mostly as an expedition of sin."

00:07:50.490 --> 00:07:53.700
I.e. a reparation for our
spiritual bad behavior.

00:07:53.700 --> 00:07:56.100
And once Christians
got gung-ho about work,

00:07:56.100 --> 00:07:58.170
criminalizing anybody with a lazy bone

00:07:58.170 --> 00:08:00.120
in their body inevitably followed.

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:01.650
In the late middle ages,

00:08:01.650 --> 00:08:04.230
idle behavior was treated as a crime,

00:08:04.230 --> 00:08:06.270
often punished with a public flogging

00:08:06.270 --> 00:08:07.680
followed by forced labor.

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:09.930
And this continued for centuries.

00:08:09.930 --> 00:08:13.800
Author, Zuzanna Ladyga, points
out that well into the 1800,

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:16.170
almost identical laws were used in Europe

00:08:16.170 --> 00:08:18.750
and the United States in times of crisis

00:08:18.750 --> 00:08:21.330
to regulate the distribution
of cheap workforces,

00:08:21.330 --> 00:08:24.660
control population mobility,
and stabilize state power.

00:08:24.660 --> 00:08:27.480
In America, they were called tramp X,

00:08:27.480 --> 00:08:29.820
and violators were forcibly engraved

00:08:29.820 --> 00:08:33.090
with intricate horizontal
tattoos on their lower backs.

00:08:33.090 --> 00:08:35.880
But while Christianity always valued work,

00:08:35.880 --> 00:08:39.630
things went into overdrive in
the 16th and 17th centuries.

00:08:39.630 --> 00:08:42.870
Two big things spurred this
productivity happy revolution.

00:08:42.870 --> 00:08:45.570
The first was the Protestant Reformation,

00:08:45.570 --> 00:08:48.660
which transformed the perception
of work by treating labor,

00:08:48.660 --> 00:08:50.130
not as a punishment,

00:08:50.130 --> 00:08:52.680
but as a virtuous pursuit in and of itself

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:54.840
to be undertaken with zeal.

00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:57.210
Specifically, Calvinists
believed that success

00:08:57.210 --> 00:09:00.390
in this lifetime was a sign
of predestined salvation.

00:09:00.390 --> 00:09:02.400
So it made sense to hustle your butt off

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:03.687
in order to prove to yourself

00:09:03.687 --> 00:09:06.450
and all your buddies that
you were heaven bound.

00:09:06.450 --> 00:09:08.610
The second big change was the advancement

00:09:08.610 --> 00:09:09.810
of the humble clock.

00:09:09.810 --> 00:09:12.000
See, according to
philosopher, George Woodcock,

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:13.680
for most of Western history,

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:17.670
time was conceived of as a
sort of flowing amorphous

00:09:17.670 --> 00:09:21.390
ethereal thing and
calculated pretty vaguely.

00:09:21.390 --> 00:09:22.650
But the invention of,

00:09:22.650 --> 00:09:24.030
and then advancements to the clock

00:09:24.030 --> 00:09:26.190
from minute hands around the late 1600

00:09:26.190 --> 00:09:28.710
to accurate secondhand
a century or so later,

00:09:28.710 --> 00:09:30.840
altered our conception of time.

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:33.360
And consequently, it
altered how much humans felt

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:35.430
they should accomplish during that time.

00:09:35.430 --> 00:09:36.577
As Woodcock writes,

00:09:36.577 --> 00:09:39.360
"The clock turns time
from a process of nature

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:43.140
into a commodity that can be
measured and bought and sold.

00:09:43.140 --> 00:09:46.710
The clock happy post-reformation
world got another boost

00:09:46.710 --> 00:09:49.440
from the enlightenment,
which among other things,

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:52.860
valorized mankind's ability
to perform productive labor.

00:09:52.860 --> 00:09:56.010
At the time labor was,
"Frequently perceived positively

00:09:56.010 --> 00:09:59.070
as a commodity and as a source
of social respectability,"

00:09:59.070 --> 00:10:01.020
according to scholar, Deborah Simonton.

00:10:01.020 --> 00:10:03.870
At the same time, the west
was undergoing intense

00:10:03.870 --> 00:10:05.370
economic growth and expansion.

00:10:05.370 --> 00:10:06.660
As markets blossomed,

00:10:06.660 --> 00:10:08.310
work became more regimented,

00:10:08.310 --> 00:10:11.790
thus time keeping became
linked to industriousness.

00:10:11.790 --> 00:10:14.190
Founding father and patron
and sent a to do list,

00:10:14.190 --> 00:10:16.950
Benjamin Franklin observed time is money.

00:10:16.950 --> 00:10:19.710
One thing on his to-do list
was definitely get syphilis

00:10:19.710 --> 00:10:21.270
because he had syphilis.

00:10:21.270 --> 00:10:24.060
Simonton explains, "Enlightenment
ideas and burgeoning

00:10:24.060 --> 00:10:28.020
entrepreneurship colluded to
improve efficiency and output."

00:10:28.020 --> 00:10:31.470
But the joy of an industrious
day's work might not have been

00:10:31.470 --> 00:10:34.050
enough on its own to
catapult American culture

00:10:34.050 --> 00:10:35.670
into a flurry of productivity.

00:10:35.670 --> 00:10:38.820
Sociologist, Max Weber
argues that the rapid rise

00:10:38.820 --> 00:10:41.040
of capitalism can be
attributed significantly

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:43.830
to the lingering power of
the Protestant Work Ethic.

00:10:43.830 --> 00:10:45.210
Remember the Protestant Work Ethic

00:10:45.210 --> 00:10:48.720
conveniently equated economic
success with moral virtue

00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:52.500
and no wealthy capitalists
were gonna be mad at that.

00:10:52.500 --> 00:10:55.980
The Protestant mindset of
discipline and regulation

00:10:55.980 --> 00:10:57.600
also facilitated the expansion

00:10:57.600 --> 00:10:59.970
of punitive policies against vagrants,

00:10:59.970 --> 00:11:02.280
including mass arrests and forced labor,

00:11:02.280 --> 00:11:05.130
which basically just meant
making life hell for vagrants,

00:11:05.130 --> 00:11:07.890
wanderers and anyone who
couldn't seem to hold down a job.

00:11:07.890 --> 00:11:10.827
That's important because a
big part of valorizing work

00:11:10.827 --> 00:11:13.140
has always been about the
elites wanting to keep

00:11:13.140 --> 00:11:14.310
the poorest busy

00:11:14.310 --> 00:11:16.560
so they wouldn't indulge
in naughty diversions

00:11:16.560 --> 00:11:18.210
like drinking or sex.

00:11:18.210 --> 00:11:20.467
It's history's favorite
short king, Napoleon wrote,

00:11:20.467 --> 00:11:24.210
"The more my people work, the
less vices they will have."

00:11:24.210 --> 00:11:27.630
Society's growing fixation on
productivity and demonization

00:11:27.630 --> 00:11:30.690
of laziness provided a secular
substitute for the religious

00:11:30.690 --> 00:11:33.150
discipline that was
falling out of fashion.

00:11:33.150 --> 00:11:36.360
The valorization of work in
America got another big boost

00:11:36.360 --> 00:11:38.550
during the Second Industrial Revolution.

00:11:38.550 --> 00:11:41.310
A combination of lingering
reformation views,

00:11:41.310 --> 00:11:43.200
Victorian morality around work ethic

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:44.370
and delayed gratification,

00:11:44.370 --> 00:11:47.760
as well as that all American
ethos of boots strapping

00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:49.890
in your way to success all can shield

00:11:49.890 --> 00:11:52.560
into a culture of intense industriousness.

00:11:52.560 --> 00:11:55.080
It was now every man's duty to work hard

00:11:55.080 --> 00:11:56.670
and produce useful things.

00:11:56.670 --> 00:11:58.110
And with production speeding up,

00:11:58.110 --> 00:12:00.150
thanks to the new fangled
mechanics of everything

00:12:00.150 --> 00:12:02.070
from the steamboat to the sewing machine,

00:12:02.070 --> 00:12:03.510
workers were quickly transported

00:12:03.510 --> 00:12:05.070
from hand making goods at home,

00:12:05.070 --> 00:12:06.510
to working in factories.

00:12:06.510 --> 00:12:08.970
We talk about this in our
video on working from home.

00:12:08.970 --> 00:12:10.350
But this moment in history is important

00:12:10.350 --> 00:12:12.120
to today's video too.

00:12:12.120 --> 00:12:15.000
Basically, early factory
workers were not exactly

00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:17.460
what you'd call well behaved.

00:12:17.460 --> 00:12:19.290
They went from making their own hours

00:12:19.290 --> 00:12:20.970
at their own homes to being subjected

00:12:20.970 --> 00:12:24.240
to grueling endless work days
and unpleasant factories.

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:27.780
Unsurprisingly, absenteeism
was a huge problem.

00:12:27.780 --> 00:12:29.970
People were very often drunk on the job,

00:12:29.970 --> 00:12:32.220
which despite what they
might have thought,

00:12:32.220 --> 00:12:33.840
didn't actually make them funnier.

00:12:33.840 --> 00:12:35.887
As historian, Daniel T. Rogers writes,

00:12:35.887 --> 00:12:38.160
"By reporting irregularly for work,

00:12:38.160 --> 00:12:40.140
moving restlessly from job to job,

00:12:40.140 --> 00:12:42.450
or engaging in slowdowns
and work restrictions,

00:12:42.450 --> 00:12:44.940
industrial laborers stubbornly resisted

00:12:44.940 --> 00:12:47.070
the new work discipline
the factory masters

00:12:47.070 --> 00:12:48.480
tried to impose upon them."

00:12:48.480 --> 00:12:51.330
Enter mechanical engineer,
Frederick W. Taylor,

00:12:51.330 --> 00:12:54.360
who became obsessed with
expediting production processes

00:12:54.360 --> 00:12:56.370
in the late 19th century factory,

00:12:56.370 --> 00:12:58.560
which basically meant wrangling workers.

00:12:58.560 --> 00:13:02.040
He realized that because of
their past as skilled craftsmen,

00:13:02.040 --> 00:13:04.680
workers possessed knowledge and dexterity

00:13:04.680 --> 00:13:06.330
that actually made them more valuable

00:13:06.330 --> 00:13:07.950
than the foreman managing them.

00:13:07.950 --> 00:13:10.650
This in Taylor's view was dangerous.

00:13:10.650 --> 00:13:12.840
It gave workers way too much power

00:13:12.840 --> 00:13:14.160
over the means of production.

00:13:14.160 --> 00:13:16.380
So he came up with a way
to increase efficiency

00:13:16.380 --> 00:13:19.500
and consolidate control much
higher up the food chain,

00:13:19.500 --> 00:13:22.980
all via a new field of study
called scientific management,

00:13:22.980 --> 00:13:24.930
which was later dubbed tailorism.

00:13:24.930 --> 00:13:26.377
As John Zeerzan writes,

00:13:26.377 --> 00:13:29.850
"Scientific management began
to take shape in the 1880s

00:13:29.850 --> 00:13:32.850
as the way to break the
workers' threatening resistance.

00:13:32.850 --> 00:13:35.400
The heart of this approach
is the systematic reduction

00:13:35.400 --> 00:13:39.000
of work into discreet, routinized tasks,

00:13:39.000 --> 00:13:42.810
totally separated from any
policy decisions about the job."

00:13:42.810 --> 00:13:45.600
Now Adam Smith had conceived
of the assembly line

00:13:45.600 --> 00:13:48.630
over a century earlier in his
book, "Wealth of nations."

00:13:48.630 --> 00:13:50.520
And such systems had already existed in

00:13:50.520 --> 00:13:52.170
American meat packing factories.

00:13:52.170 --> 00:13:55.110
But Taylor put this process on steroids,

00:13:55.110 --> 00:13:58.500
breaking production into
minuscule individual tasks,

00:13:58.500 --> 00:14:02.400
and assigning workers the
same task to do over and over

00:14:02.400 --> 00:14:04.560
again at the fastest possible speed,

00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:06.450
which is not at all a recipe

00:14:06.450 --> 00:14:09.000
for slowly losing your (beep) mind.

00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:11.490
As Taylor's methods were widely adopted,

00:14:11.490 --> 00:14:15.360
workers went from producing,
say, a shoe or a carriage

00:14:15.360 --> 00:14:16.320
at their own pace,

00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:17.970
to performing what Rogers calls,

00:14:17.970 --> 00:14:20.910
specialized repetitious machine paced

00:14:20.910 --> 00:14:23.550
and often deadingly simple actions.

00:14:23.550 --> 00:14:26.880
What's more, workers were now
de-skilled and disempowered.

00:14:26.880 --> 00:14:29.670
They didn't know how the
wider factory worked.

00:14:29.670 --> 00:14:33.480
They only knew how to
perform their specific tasks.

00:14:33.480 --> 00:14:35.580
Effectively, the first major methods

00:14:35.580 --> 00:14:37.770
of increasing productivity
were largely about

00:14:37.770 --> 00:14:40.680
management imposing control on workers.

00:14:40.680 --> 00:14:43.500
Taylor's influence was
vast and he wasn't alone.

00:14:43.500 --> 00:14:44.760
Other efficiency experts,

00:14:44.760 --> 00:14:47.100
most notably Frank and Lilian Gilbreth,

00:14:47.100 --> 00:14:49.830
the real family behind
cheaper by the dozen,

00:14:49.830 --> 00:14:51.510
sought to expedite productivity.

00:14:51.510 --> 00:14:53.730
Specifically by analyzing the (beep)

00:14:53.730 --> 00:14:55.650
out of workers' physical movements.

00:14:55.650 --> 00:14:58.890
They broke down every work
task into individual motions,

00:14:58.890 --> 00:15:01.800
which they call the units of therbligs,

00:15:01.800 --> 00:15:04.110
i.e. Gilbert spelled backwards.

00:15:04.110 --> 00:15:06.810
They filmed workers
performing acts of labor,

00:15:06.810 --> 00:15:09.360
and studied each frame
attempting to cut out

00:15:09.360 --> 00:15:12.067
unnecessary motions in
search of what they called

00:15:12.067 --> 00:15:14.790
"the one best way of
performing each task".

00:15:14.790 --> 00:15:17.610
Notably, Lillian Gilbreth's
work after her husband,

00:15:17.610 --> 00:15:21.210
Frank's death was focused on
promoting domestic science,

00:15:21.210 --> 00:15:25.050
i.e. bringing the art of
efficiency into the household.

00:15:25.050 --> 00:15:26.850
As factories grew more efficient,

00:15:26.850 --> 00:15:27.840
the thinking went,

00:15:27.840 --> 00:15:30.870
why shouldn't housewives also
benefit from these methods?

00:15:30.870 --> 00:15:33.540
Gilbreth and other home
efficiency experts taught women

00:15:33.540 --> 00:15:36.630
everything from the best
posture for washing dishes,

00:15:36.630 --> 00:15:38.460
to the fastest way to can baby food,

00:15:38.460 --> 00:15:40.680
and encouraged them to
find ways to maximize

00:15:40.680 --> 00:15:43.620
their efficiency by reducing
the motions required

00:15:43.620 --> 00:15:45.150
to perform each task.

00:15:45.150 --> 00:15:47.850
Another thing that Gilbert's
realized was that worker

00:15:47.850 --> 00:15:50.400
psychology that is their
temperament and contentment

00:15:50.400 --> 00:15:52.350
was also essential to productivity.

00:15:52.350 --> 00:15:55.950
In what was basically the first
rumbling of human resources,

00:15:55.950 --> 00:15:57.630
they encouraged employers to make

00:15:57.630 --> 00:15:59.640
workers' surroundings more pleasant.

00:15:59.640 --> 00:16:01.920
Provide magazines for them
to read and even set up

00:16:01.920 --> 00:16:05.310
suggestion boxes where workers
could make other requests.

00:16:05.310 --> 00:16:08.670
The Silicon Valley startups
that feel more like cool coffee

00:16:08.670 --> 00:16:12.270
shops than offices have their
roots in this understanding

00:16:12.270 --> 00:16:15.390
that happy workers tend
to work harder and faster.

00:16:15.390 --> 00:16:16.437
Helped along by tailorism

00:16:16.437 --> 00:16:18.600
and the production
needs of two World Wars,

00:16:18.600 --> 00:16:22.140
productivity rates in America
began soaring in the 1920s

00:16:22.140 --> 00:16:24.630
and continued that trend up into the 70s.

00:16:24.630 --> 00:16:26.550
And despite the glaring issues

00:16:26.550 --> 00:16:28.050
with treating people like machines,

00:16:28.050 --> 00:16:29.760
there seemed to be obvious benefits

00:16:29.760 --> 00:16:31.170
to increasing productivity.

00:16:31.170 --> 00:16:34.110
As Frayne explains, in
the early 20th century,

00:16:34.110 --> 00:16:36.780
business leaders assumed
that the natural results

00:16:36.780 --> 00:16:38.520
of improved production techniques

00:16:38.520 --> 00:16:40.110
would be an increased in free time.

00:16:40.110 --> 00:16:41.910
Economist, John Maynard Kings predicted

00:16:41.910 --> 00:16:45.420
that his grandchildren
would work 15-hour weeks.

00:16:45.420 --> 00:16:48.060
Kings' prediction seems
naive in hindsight,

00:16:48.060 --> 00:16:50.700
but when he was writing
in 1930, it made sense.

00:16:50.700 --> 00:16:54.840
After all, production had been
rising in unprecedented 5.3%

00:16:54.840 --> 00:16:56.460
a year over the previous decade.

00:16:56.460 --> 00:16:58.950
How many buttons and
radios and toothbrushes

00:16:58.950 --> 00:17:00.750
could one country need?

00:17:00.750 --> 00:17:03.420
And there was precedent for
shortening the work week.

00:17:03.420 --> 00:17:05.130
A scour of Benjamin Hunnicutt explains,

00:17:05.130 --> 00:17:07.950
in the 1920s, workweeks had
been steadily decreasing

00:17:07.950 --> 00:17:11.010
from 70 hours or more in the early 1800,

00:17:11.010 --> 00:17:13.980
to a more manageable
50 hours by the 1920s.

00:17:13.980 --> 00:17:15.870
And there was reason to
think that newly increased

00:17:15.870 --> 00:17:18.720
productivity would lead
to even more leisure time.

00:17:18.720 --> 00:17:20.280
But faced with this opportunity

00:17:20.280 --> 00:17:21.960
to make their workers' lives happier,

00:17:21.960 --> 00:17:23.760
more relaxing, and more fulfilling,

00:17:23.760 --> 00:17:26.370
businesses said, actually,
yeah, we're good.

00:17:26.370 --> 00:17:27.501
We don't need to do that.

00:17:27.501 --> 00:17:28.890
We're just gonna make them keep working.

00:17:28.890 --> 00:17:29.723
Why?

00:17:29.723 --> 00:17:31.747
As social researcher,
David Frayne explains,

00:17:31.747 --> 00:17:34.260
"The idea of a less work and more leisure

00:17:34.260 --> 00:17:36.060
was completely counterintuitive

00:17:36.060 --> 00:17:38.070
to the dogma of economic growth."

00:17:38.070 --> 00:17:39.750
Basically now that they can produce goods

00:17:39.750 --> 00:17:41.130
faster and more cheaply,

00:17:41.130 --> 00:17:43.650
instead of letting workers take a siesta,

00:17:43.650 --> 00:17:46.500
they decided to just produce
a (beep) ton more goods

00:17:46.500 --> 00:17:47.820
and maximize profits.

00:17:47.820 --> 00:17:49.920
Then there was the whole
more morality side of things.

00:17:49.920 --> 00:17:51.600
Rich people don't love it

00:17:51.600 --> 00:17:53.520
when poor people have too much free time.

00:17:53.520 --> 00:17:56.940
Sigmont Freud even worried that
if labor hours were reduced,

00:17:56.940 --> 00:17:59.880
emancipated working class
Americans would fall into

00:17:59.880 --> 00:18:01.860
an orgiastic rule of nature

00:18:01.860 --> 00:18:03.990
and indulge in their
darkest predilections,

00:18:03.990 --> 00:18:06.030
like incest, cannibalism, and murder.

00:18:06.030 --> 00:18:09.000
Companies wanted to keep growing
and elites wanted workers

00:18:09.000 --> 00:18:11.400
to keep logging long
hours in the factories.

00:18:11.400 --> 00:18:14.400
As a result, America was
producing massive quantities

00:18:14.400 --> 00:18:15.510
of consumer goods.

00:18:15.510 --> 00:18:19.080
So what do you do with a
big surplus of products?

00:18:19.080 --> 00:18:21.210
You start hawking that
(beep) via what historian,

00:18:21.210 --> 00:18:24.210
Edward S. Cowdrick, dubbed
the new economic gospel

00:18:24.210 --> 00:18:25.980
of consumption of the 1920s.

00:18:25.980 --> 00:18:28.197
Or as scholar, Kathi Weeks writes,

00:18:28.197 --> 00:18:30.420
"Consumption merged as an
essential economic practice.

00:18:30.420 --> 00:18:32.790
Producers were expected to do double duty

00:18:32.790 --> 00:18:34.950
as aesthetically indulgent consumers."

00:18:34.950 --> 00:18:37.410
But how are people supposed
to be good consumers

00:18:37.410 --> 00:18:39.330
if they weren't making very much money?

00:18:39.330 --> 00:18:41.160
Henry Ford solved that puzzle

00:18:41.160 --> 00:18:44.130
when he started paying
his workers $5 a day,

00:18:44.130 --> 00:18:45.330
twice the market rate.

00:18:45.330 --> 00:18:47.220
While not his primary motivation,

00:18:47.220 --> 00:18:49.740
this did have the effect of
making his cars more affordable

00:18:49.740 --> 00:18:51.930
to his workers, thus expanding his market.

00:18:51.930 --> 00:18:53.820
It was a huge success.

00:18:53.820 --> 00:18:56.430
Ford double his profits in just two years.

00:18:56.430 --> 00:18:58.230
And this quickly convinced other employers

00:18:58.230 --> 00:19:00.240
to follow suit and raise wages.

00:19:00.240 --> 00:19:02.820
According to labor
economist, Harley Shaiken,

00:19:02.820 --> 00:19:05.580
what that gave us was an
industrial middle class

00:19:05.580 --> 00:19:08.220
and an economy that was
driven by consumer demand.

00:19:08.220 --> 00:19:11.820
Basically for the first time,
people could buy fun things.

00:19:11.820 --> 00:19:13.530
Of course, it's no coincidence

00:19:13.530 --> 00:19:15.720
that just as American businesses had way

00:19:15.720 --> 00:19:16.890
more products to sell,

00:19:16.890 --> 00:19:19.890
we saw the simultaneous
emergence of modern advertising

00:19:19.890 --> 00:19:22.080
spurred on by the psychological approach

00:19:22.080 --> 00:19:24.540
of Freud's nephew, Edward Bernays.

00:19:24.540 --> 00:19:27.210
Advertisements evolved from
their traditional practice

00:19:27.210 --> 00:19:28.950
of appealing to people with reason,

00:19:28.950 --> 00:19:31.770
i.e. bragging about the high
quality of their toothpaste,

00:19:31.770 --> 00:19:34.200
and instead started relying on emotional

00:19:34.200 --> 00:19:35.550
and symbolic appeals,

00:19:35.550 --> 00:19:39.030
i.e. promising that this
toothpaste will get you laid.

00:19:39.030 --> 00:19:41.940
This approach was necessary
because sellers with massive

00:19:41.940 --> 00:19:44.070
surpluses were no longer simply catering

00:19:44.070 --> 00:19:45.570
to consumers' basic needs.

00:19:45.570 --> 00:19:48.600
Now they were creating
entirely new desires.

00:19:48.600 --> 00:19:51.810
As Bernays wrote, "Today,
supply must actively seek

00:19:51.810 --> 00:19:54.090
to create its corresponding demand.

00:19:54.090 --> 00:19:55.320
And cannot afford to wait until

00:19:55.320 --> 00:19:57.150
the public asks for its product.

00:19:57.150 --> 00:20:00.240
It must maintain constant
touch through advertising

00:20:00.240 --> 00:20:04.260
and propaganda to assure
itself the continuous demand."

00:20:04.260 --> 00:20:07.380
Such began a virtuous cycle
of people becoming more

00:20:07.380 --> 00:20:09.600
productive while earning higher wages.

00:20:09.600 --> 00:20:12.210
And thus businesses had
more products to sell,

00:20:12.210 --> 00:20:14.370
which they then pedal to their workers,

00:20:14.370 --> 00:20:17.160
because they could now
afford to buy (beep) for fun.

00:20:17.160 --> 00:20:19.620
So everybody wins and it all worked out.

00:20:19.620 --> 00:20:20.880
This is the end of the video.

00:20:20.880 --> 00:20:22.440
Of course it's not, it gets bad again.

00:20:22.440 --> 00:20:25.290
Now, ever since productivity
became a driving concern

00:20:25.290 --> 00:20:26.970
around the turn of the 20th century,

00:20:26.970 --> 00:20:30.000
it had mostly been a
top-down process of managers

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:31.860
spurring productivity in their workers

00:20:31.860 --> 00:20:35.790
through surveillance,
discipline, and encouragement.

00:20:35.790 --> 00:20:38.190
That was about to shift
dramatically in the 50s

00:20:38.190 --> 00:20:40.830
and 60s with the rise of knowledge work,

00:20:40.830 --> 00:20:43.230
i.e. work that forces you
to think for a living.

00:20:43.230 --> 00:20:45.450
Because knowledge work
was more complex, varied,

00:20:45.450 --> 00:20:48.390
and nuanced than say, a factory
assembly line of workers

00:20:48.390 --> 00:20:49.920
mass producing widgets,

00:20:49.920 --> 00:20:51.360
productivity started becoming more

00:20:51.360 --> 00:20:53.490
difficult to monitor and to quantify.

00:20:53.490 --> 00:20:56.550
This was about to change
the entire way we conceive

00:20:56.550 --> 00:20:57.900
of getting stuff done.

00:20:57.900 --> 00:20:59.820
At the time, management consultant,

00:20:59.820 --> 00:21:02.460
Peter Drucker argued
that personal autonomy

00:21:02.460 --> 00:21:04.140
would become the defining feature

00:21:04.140 --> 00:21:06.060
of the new knowledge-based corporate world

00:21:06.060 --> 00:21:08.520
as individual workers became
responsible for tracking

00:21:08.520 --> 00:21:09.660
their own productivity.

00:21:09.660 --> 00:21:11.730
He wrote, "The knowledge worker cannot

00:21:11.730 --> 00:21:14.070
be supervised closely or in detail.

00:21:14.070 --> 00:21:15.930
He must direct himself."

00:21:15.930 --> 00:21:17.580
As the impetus fell on individuals

00:21:17.580 --> 00:21:19.290
to self-manage their productivity,

00:21:19.290 --> 00:21:21.180
many sought advice on how the heck

00:21:21.180 --> 00:21:22.470
they were supposed to do that.

00:21:22.470 --> 00:21:25.020
Fittingly, this is when
productivity self-help books

00:21:25.020 --> 00:21:27.300
first became a fixture
of the book industry.

00:21:27.300 --> 00:21:28.350
The trend kicked off

00:21:28.350 --> 00:21:31.290
with James McCay's "The
Management of Time" in 1959,

00:21:31.290 --> 00:21:32.347
which featured quotes like,

00:21:32.347 --> 00:21:34.920
"Tomorrow you promise
yourself will be different,

00:21:34.920 --> 00:21:38.400
yet tomorrow is too often
a repetition of today."

00:21:38.400 --> 00:21:39.690
Beginning in the 60s,

00:21:39.690 --> 00:21:42.240
the productivity guru emerged

00:21:42.240 --> 00:21:44.580
as a trustworthy figure offering firm,

00:21:44.580 --> 00:21:46.020
but reassuring advice.

00:21:46.020 --> 00:21:48.810
Weeks explains that
gurus typically suggest

00:21:48.810 --> 00:21:52.200
some kind of reformed
daily routine to insert

00:21:52.200 --> 00:21:54.150
ritual awareness into one's day,

00:21:54.150 --> 00:21:55.710
much like a religious refrain.

00:21:55.710 --> 00:21:58.410
Importantly, the personal
productivity industry

00:21:58.410 --> 00:22:01.140
often flourishes during
economic downturns.

00:22:01.140 --> 00:22:03.240
In the recession of the mid 1970s,

00:22:03.240 --> 00:22:06.390
books like "How to Get
Control of Your Life and Time"

00:22:06.390 --> 00:22:09.300
urged anxious workers to make
the most of their coffee break

00:22:09.300 --> 00:22:11.640
by learning a new word
from their dictionary.

00:22:11.640 --> 00:22:12.960
By the early 90s,

00:22:12.960 --> 00:22:14.790
yet another recession had books

00:22:14.790 --> 00:22:17.280
like "Self-Discipline in 10 days"

00:22:17.280 --> 00:22:20.100
promising to help you go
from thinking to doing.

00:22:20.100 --> 00:22:22.800
This literature was serving
an important purpose.

00:22:22.800 --> 00:22:25.200
It offered hope for
workers pursuing success

00:22:25.200 --> 00:22:27.420
in an increasingly competitive job market,

00:22:27.420 --> 00:22:29.280
right as layoffs and corporate downsizing

00:22:29.280 --> 00:22:30.690
were becoming the new normal.

00:22:30.690 --> 00:22:33.150
And weeks argues that over
the course of the greed

00:22:33.150 --> 00:22:36.877
is good 80s, and the
dial-up sound dominated 90s,

00:22:36.877 --> 00:22:39.810
"Productivity took on the
thrust of Puritan principles

00:22:39.810 --> 00:22:41.520
that could be embraced either adjacent to

00:22:41.520 --> 00:22:43.200
or in spite of religious belief

00:22:43.200 --> 00:22:44.940
because it holds the same qualities

00:22:44.940 --> 00:22:46.350
of disciplined practice."

00:22:46.350 --> 00:22:48.150
As productivity solidified itself

00:22:48.150 --> 00:22:50.250
as the individual impetus of each worker,

00:22:50.250 --> 00:22:52.830
busyness started to be seen as an ideal.

00:22:52.830 --> 00:22:53.970
And in recent years,

00:22:53.970 --> 00:22:56.460
it's even become an
aspirational status symbol.

00:22:56.460 --> 00:22:58.680
According to researcher, Sylvia Plaza.

00:22:58.680 --> 00:23:00.450
In pursuit of such status,

00:23:00.450 --> 00:23:01.770
we've all become accustomed

00:23:01.770 --> 00:23:03.720
to managing our own productivity.

00:23:03.720 --> 00:23:05.100
Some of this is circumstantial.

00:23:05.100 --> 00:23:07.410
The job market is more
competitive than ever.

00:23:07.410 --> 00:23:09.240
Wages haven't kept up with inflation.

00:23:09.240 --> 00:23:11.670
Workplace expectations
are at an all time high,

00:23:11.670 --> 00:23:15.060
and we're taught to feel
lucky to even have a job.

00:23:15.060 --> 00:23:17.220
All these circumstances combined to create

00:23:17.220 --> 00:23:19.530
a profound anxiety amongst workers.

00:23:19.530 --> 00:23:20.730
And guess what?

00:23:20.730 --> 00:23:22.260
That's great for business.

00:23:22.260 --> 00:23:23.610
Philosopher, Byang-Chul Han,

00:23:23.610 --> 00:23:26.160
explains that anxiety
increases productivity,

00:23:26.160 --> 00:23:28.627
while political theorist,
Neil Vallelly adds,

00:23:28.627 --> 00:23:30.690
"An anxious subject works harder,

00:23:30.690 --> 00:23:33.090
partly at a fear of losing their job."

00:23:33.090 --> 00:23:35.970
In short, we're obsessed with
productivity in large part

00:23:35.970 --> 00:23:38.700
because, well, we have to be.

00:23:38.700 --> 00:23:41.610
We live in a time of intense
precarity and competition.

00:23:41.610 --> 00:23:44.070
And the stability of many
career paths is haunted

00:23:44.070 --> 00:23:46.080
by ghost of future automation.

00:23:46.080 --> 00:23:48.660
Industrial Revolution
era efficiency experts

00:23:48.660 --> 00:23:51.390
were mostly concerned with
disciplining workers' bodies

00:23:51.390 --> 00:23:53.400
to make them behave more like machines.

00:23:53.400 --> 00:23:54.960
But for today's knowledge workers,

00:23:54.960 --> 00:23:57.720
Han notes, "Productivity
is not to be enhanced

00:23:57.720 --> 00:23:59.640
by overcoming physical resistance

00:23:59.640 --> 00:24:03.180
so much as by optimizing
psychic or mental processes."

00:24:03.180 --> 00:24:06.720
And now we're the ones
optimizing ourselves.

00:24:06.720 --> 00:24:09.750
This is arguably an essential
feature of neoliberalism,

00:24:09.750 --> 00:24:12.270
i.e. a liberal capitalist ideology

00:24:12.270 --> 00:24:15.660
that emphasizes free markets
and individual responsibility.

00:24:15.660 --> 00:24:18.120
As sociologists, Deborah Lupton notes,

00:24:18.120 --> 00:24:21.540
neoliberalism tends to
favor soft over hard power.

00:24:21.540 --> 00:24:24.540
Writing that, "Instead of
relying on coercive measures

00:24:24.540 --> 00:24:26.550
in managing and regulating their citizens,

00:24:26.550 --> 00:24:29.760
neoliberal political systems
invest faith in the voluntary

00:24:29.760 --> 00:24:32.790
take-up of imperatives by
the citizens themselves.

00:24:32.790 --> 00:24:34.650
Citizens believe that certain acts

00:24:34.650 --> 00:24:36.300
are in their own best interest

00:24:36.300 --> 00:24:38.340
or are integral to ideal selfhood."

00:24:38.340 --> 00:24:40.920
She cites productivity
as a prominent imperative

00:24:40.920 --> 00:24:43.230
taking up by people in
pursuit of self-betterment.

00:24:43.230 --> 00:24:45.240
Han contends that such a fixation

00:24:45.240 --> 00:24:47.940
is actually a form of self-oppression.

00:24:47.940 --> 00:24:50.340
He writes, "As an entrepreneur of himself,

00:24:50.340 --> 00:24:52.380
the neoliberal achievement subject engages

00:24:52.380 --> 00:24:54.390
in auto-exploitation willingly,

00:24:54.390 --> 00:24:55.830
and even passionately."

00:24:55.830 --> 00:24:59.490
As such, he notes, "Perpetual
self-optimization represents

00:24:59.490 --> 00:25:02.310
nothing so much as a highly efficient mode

00:25:02.310 --> 00:25:04.590
of domination and exploitation.

00:25:04.590 --> 00:25:05.917
And in this case he says,

00:25:05.917 --> 00:25:08.940
"The psyche itself is being exploited."

00:25:08.940 --> 00:25:11.970
So while Taylor had to implement
his time-saving techniques

00:25:11.970 --> 00:25:15.090
in a top down way to
wrangle disgruntled, drunk,

00:25:15.090 --> 00:25:17.130
and decidedly ungrateful workers,

00:25:17.130 --> 00:25:20.490
we now willingly impose Pomodoro Timers,

00:25:20.490 --> 00:25:21.750
5:00 AM wake up calls,

00:25:21.750 --> 00:25:25.140
and expensive neurotropic
regimens on ourselves.

00:25:25.140 --> 00:25:27.930
As such, the worker
becomes what Hong calls

00:25:27.930 --> 00:25:31.590
a self-illuminating
self-surveilling subject.

00:25:31.590 --> 00:25:32.910
He argues that we're functionally

00:25:32.910 --> 00:25:34.860
in a panopticon of our own making,

00:25:34.860 --> 00:25:38.220
one where there is no difference
between guard and inmate.

00:25:38.220 --> 00:25:41.850
Put differently, the guard
or vigilant manager is living

00:25:41.850 --> 00:25:44.010
inside our head rent free.

00:25:44.010 --> 00:25:46.740
As we've seen the religious
zeal behind personal

00:25:46.740 --> 00:25:49.050
productivity can be
traced all the way back

00:25:49.050 --> 00:25:51.870
to capitalism's spiritual
roots and Calvinism,

00:25:51.870 --> 00:25:53.250
but as Weeks points out,

00:25:53.250 --> 00:25:56.190
today's call to productivity
differs from other religions

00:25:56.190 --> 00:25:58.680
because it is entirely inwardly-oriented.

00:25:58.680 --> 00:26:01.980
It's a total devotion to one's
self and one's achievement.

00:26:01.980 --> 00:26:04.320
And it's a pretty empty ethos.

00:26:04.320 --> 00:26:07.140
In our obsession with
maximizing our output,

00:26:07.140 --> 00:26:11.340
the actual value of that output
becomes almost irrelevant.

00:26:11.340 --> 00:26:13.860
There's no driving
purpose behind the concept

00:26:13.860 --> 00:26:15.120
of pure productivity,

00:26:15.120 --> 00:26:17.250
other than a very simple market logic

00:26:17.250 --> 00:26:20.070
that any production is good production.

00:26:20.070 --> 00:26:21.907
As anthropologist, David Graver writes,

00:26:21.907 --> 00:26:24.480
"We have become a
civilization based on work,

00:26:24.480 --> 00:26:26.820
as an end and meaning in itself."

00:26:26.820 --> 00:26:30.300
Graver argues that many of
today's workers are toiling away

00:26:30.300 --> 00:26:33.560
at bull (beep) jobs defined
by meaningless work.

00:26:33.560 --> 00:26:34.650
In such an environment,

00:26:34.650 --> 00:26:36.630
it's no wonder that, as Greg notes,

00:26:36.630 --> 00:26:39.960
productivity becomes, "A
framework for knowing what to do

00:26:39.960 --> 00:26:42.840
in the absence of a guiding
principle for doing it."

00:26:42.840 --> 00:26:45.600
Our quest for productivity
arguably affords us

00:26:45.600 --> 00:26:48.780
a sense of control in
a fast paced workplace.

00:26:48.780 --> 00:26:50.257
As Greg explains,

00:26:50.257 --> 00:26:53.550
"Time management celebrates
command and control.

00:26:53.550 --> 00:26:57.120
The idea of time sovereignty
holds faith in the idea

00:26:57.120 --> 00:27:00.420
that time is territory
that can be conquered."

00:27:00.420 --> 00:27:02.940
We can't do anything about
next month's layoffs,

00:27:02.940 --> 00:27:05.880
but (beep) damn it, we
can milk every last second

00:27:05.880 --> 00:27:07.020
out of our workday.

00:27:07.020 --> 00:27:09.060
Conquering time comforts us

00:27:09.060 --> 00:27:11.220
in an era of profound insecurity.

00:27:11.220 --> 00:27:13.950
We feel an affirming twang
of self-determination

00:27:13.950 --> 00:27:16.200
every time we skip lunch
with our work piles

00:27:16.200 --> 00:27:17.400
to eat at our desk.

00:27:17.400 --> 00:27:18.233
In this way,

00:27:18.233 --> 00:27:20.857
Greg sees time management
as inherently isolating.

00:27:20.857 --> 00:27:23.430
"A form of training through
which workers become

00:27:23.430 --> 00:27:26.310
capable of the evermore
daring acts of solitude

00:27:26.310 --> 00:27:30.000
and ruthlessness necessary to
produce career competence."

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:33.300
And all of this focus on our
own productivity has the effect

00:27:33.300 --> 00:27:36.150
of distracting us from the
very reasons we're pushed

00:27:36.150 --> 00:27:38.610
to be so productive in the first place.

00:27:38.610 --> 00:27:39.577
As Han writes,

00:27:39.577 --> 00:27:41.760
"Even class struggle has transformed

00:27:41.760 --> 00:27:44.040
into an inner struggle against oneself."

00:27:44.040 --> 00:27:46.650
When we're pushing ourselves
to be more productive,

00:27:46.650 --> 00:27:49.770
we're not questioning or
agitating against the systems

00:27:49.770 --> 00:27:52.350
that have allowed America's
sweeping productivity gains

00:27:52.350 --> 00:27:54.270
to mostly flow to the top.

00:27:54.270 --> 00:27:57.060
Instead, we're trying to
control our circumstances

00:27:57.060 --> 00:27:59.040
by controlling our brains,

00:27:59.040 --> 00:28:01.710
kind of feels like trying to
stop the Titanic from sinking

00:28:01.710 --> 00:28:03.750
by making sure that
your personal life fest

00:28:03.750 --> 00:28:04.800
is on real tight.

00:28:04.800 --> 00:28:05.633
In this way,

00:28:05.633 --> 00:28:08.850
the culture of productivity
is inherently alienating.

00:28:08.850 --> 00:28:09.945
As Greg notes,

00:28:09.945 --> 00:28:13.320
Productivity's inward focus
further entrenches the erasure

00:28:13.320 --> 00:28:16.620
of collective thinking that
the first efficiency engineers

00:28:16.620 --> 00:28:17.550
sought to accomplish."

00:28:17.550 --> 00:28:20.160
Just as Taylor took the
factories' collective production

00:28:20.160 --> 00:28:23.340
processes and divided it
into individual tasks,

00:28:23.340 --> 00:28:26.190
our contemporary
productivity obsession makes

00:28:26.190 --> 00:28:30.090
corporate success a matter
of individual responsibility.

00:28:30.090 --> 00:28:33.570
And then it pits those
individuals against one another.

00:28:33.570 --> 00:28:36.090
As philosopher, Pierre Bourdieu observes,

00:28:36.090 --> 00:28:39.150
under neoliberalism,
competition that once existed

00:28:39.150 --> 00:28:41.910
between say, two competing corporations,

00:28:41.910 --> 00:28:44.010
has increasingly shifted to competition

00:28:44.010 --> 00:28:47.250
amongst individuals within the same firms.

00:28:47.250 --> 00:28:50.220
Today, your real rival
is just one cubicle over.

00:28:50.220 --> 00:28:51.120
At the same time,

00:28:51.120 --> 00:28:53.550
the atomized nature of modern work culture

00:28:53.550 --> 00:28:55.980
perpetuates another neoliberal truism,

00:28:55.980 --> 00:28:59.220
that any individual's failures
come from their own moral

00:28:59.220 --> 00:29:00.660
or intellectual shortcomings.

00:29:00.660 --> 00:29:02.910
Han argues that, "People who fail

00:29:02.910 --> 00:29:04.650
in the neoliberal achievement-society

00:29:04.650 --> 00:29:07.260
see themselves as
responsible for their lot

00:29:07.260 --> 00:29:09.420
and feels shame instead of questioning

00:29:09.420 --> 00:29:11.130
society or the system."

00:29:11.130 --> 00:29:14.550
Maybe your ADHD makes keeping
up with hundreds of emails

00:29:14.550 --> 00:29:16.800
a day in insurmountable challenge.

00:29:16.800 --> 00:29:20.520
Maybe your dyslexia makes
copy editing legal documents

00:29:20.520 --> 00:29:22.950
a slow and laborious task.

00:29:22.950 --> 00:29:25.860
Or maybe you're just bored of
spreadsheets and bureaucracy

00:29:25.860 --> 00:29:27.330
and stupid corporate jargon.

00:29:27.330 --> 00:29:28.800
Damn, you sound pretty lazy.

00:29:28.800 --> 00:29:31.860
So don't come knocking on my
door, asking for handouts.

00:29:31.860 --> 00:29:34.470
In sum, anyone who doesn't commit to fully

00:29:34.470 --> 00:29:37.410
optimizing themselves
is seen as inadequate,

00:29:37.410 --> 00:29:38.550
even delinquent.

00:29:38.550 --> 00:29:39.383
As graver notes,

00:29:39.383 --> 00:29:41.610
"We've come to believe that
men and women who do not work

00:29:41.610 --> 00:29:45.150
harder than they wish at jobs
they do not particularly enjoy

00:29:45.150 --> 00:29:48.180
are bad people unworthy of love, care,

00:29:48.180 --> 00:29:49.920
or assistance from their communities.

00:29:49.920 --> 00:29:52.260
It is as if we have
collectively acquiesced

00:29:52.260 --> 00:29:53.730
to our own enslavement."

00:29:53.730 --> 00:29:57.720
So hows an overly productive
society supposed to cope?

00:29:57.720 --> 00:29:59.460
Recently an influx of new books

00:29:59.460 --> 00:30:01.500
like Jenny, Odell's "How to Do Nothing",

00:30:01.500 --> 00:30:03.930
have squarely taken on
productivity culture.

00:30:03.930 --> 00:30:05.550
Odell argues that cultivating

00:30:05.550 --> 00:30:07.650
decidedly unproductive practices,

00:30:07.650 --> 00:30:10.440
in her case, spending time
at parks watching birds,

00:30:10.440 --> 00:30:13.320
is a crucial step in taking
back our own attention,

00:30:13.320 --> 00:30:15.360
and building forms of resistance

00:30:15.360 --> 00:30:17.790
to capitalism's limitless
drive for production.

00:30:17.790 --> 00:30:20.107
This argument suggests
that, as Weeks puts it,

00:30:20.107 --> 00:30:24.120
"There is a value of non-work
time as a resource for social,

00:30:24.120 --> 00:30:27.120
cultural, and political
projects of transvaluation."

00:30:27.120 --> 00:30:30.270
That is to say we have to
take time off from work

00:30:30.270 --> 00:30:33.540
in order to have the time to
question whether work should be

00:30:33.540 --> 00:30:36.480
our highest priority in the first place.

00:30:36.480 --> 00:30:38.910
Scholar, Brian O'Conner
furthers this point,

00:30:38.910 --> 00:30:41.310
arguing that there's a profound freedom

00:30:41.310 --> 00:30:42.600
in preserving idle time

00:30:42.600 --> 00:30:45.360
explicitly not defined by achievement.

00:30:45.360 --> 00:30:48.390
He argues that idleness
is, "An implicit resistance

00:30:48.390 --> 00:30:51.660
to specific recommendations
about how one ought to live:

00:30:51.660 --> 00:30:55.260
The need for progress, prestige,
or success through work.

00:30:55.260 --> 00:30:57.690
And it is also the
pleasure that we experience

00:30:57.690 --> 00:31:00.570
when we are free to live
Without those expectations."

00:31:00.570 --> 00:31:02.130
Maybe we could learn a thing or two from

00:31:02.130 --> 00:31:04.560
all those day drunk
factory workers after all.

00:31:04.560 --> 00:31:07.860
But if being idle for even
a minute sounds awful,

00:31:07.860 --> 00:31:09.570
Greg proposes a different way

00:31:09.570 --> 00:31:11.250
to radicalize productivity culture.

00:31:11.250 --> 00:31:14.400
She writes that we need
to move our aspirations

00:31:14.400 --> 00:31:16.440
for productivity from the corporate

00:31:16.440 --> 00:31:17.730
to the collective interest.

00:31:17.730 --> 00:31:20.610
Productive atmospheres
are truly revolutionary

00:31:20.610 --> 00:31:23.370
when they initiate a form
of collective solidarity

00:31:23.370 --> 00:31:25.140
that is not dependent on labor."

00:31:25.140 --> 00:31:28.530
And that sounds suspiciously
similar to something Amazon

00:31:28.530 --> 00:31:31.200
and Starbucks workers are
currently fighting for.

00:31:31.200 --> 00:31:35.160
Now arguably, personal
productivity is an internalization

00:31:35.160 --> 00:31:36.930
of the obsessive growth mindset

00:31:36.930 --> 00:31:39.330
that is long dominated
American economic life.

00:31:39.330 --> 00:31:41.790
We should produce not
because it's valuable,

00:31:41.790 --> 00:31:43.410
but because we can.

00:31:43.410 --> 00:31:46.440
But just as dissenters have
reacted to productivity culture

00:31:46.440 --> 00:31:48.060
on an individual level,

00:31:48.060 --> 00:31:51.090
we see a similar tide
rising on a societal level

00:31:51.090 --> 00:31:53.310
with the emergence of
the degrowth movement.

00:31:53.310 --> 00:31:54.840
Drawing on ecoliberalism,

00:31:54.840 --> 00:31:56.730
which seeks to balance individualism

00:31:56.730 --> 00:31:58.980
and industry with ecological concerns,

00:31:58.980 --> 00:32:01.590
degrowth advocates argue
that pursuing unchecked

00:32:01.590 --> 00:32:04.500
economic growth is antithetical
to protecting the planet

00:32:04.500 --> 00:32:06.420
from environmental catastrophe.

00:32:06.420 --> 00:32:08.970
They advocate for vastly
ramping down production

00:32:08.970 --> 00:32:10.740
and consumption and prioritizing

00:32:10.740 --> 00:32:13.620
social and ecological wellbeing instead.

00:32:13.620 --> 00:32:16.177
As economic anthropologist,
Jason Hickel writes,

00:32:16.177 --> 00:32:17.430
"We need to slow it all down.

00:32:17.430 --> 00:32:21.420
Slow down the mad pace of
extraction, production, and waste,

00:32:21.420 --> 00:32:23.750
and slow down the mad pace of our lives."

00:32:23.750 --> 00:32:26.070
In this way, the de-growth
movement compels us

00:32:26.070 --> 00:32:29.130
to also question our personal
fixation on productivity,

00:32:29.130 --> 00:32:31.950
which is really just the atomized version

00:32:31.950 --> 00:32:34.260
of society's ceaseless growth mindset.

00:32:34.260 --> 00:32:36.600
And this seems like a healthy thing to do,

00:32:36.600 --> 00:32:39.630
because if productivity culture
isn't making us happier,

00:32:39.630 --> 00:32:41.730
and if endlessly growing production

00:32:41.730 --> 00:32:43.620
is slowly destroying the planet,

00:32:43.620 --> 00:32:45.780
perhaps it's time to think
a little less about getting

00:32:45.780 --> 00:32:49.770
things done and a little more
about getting things right.

00:32:49.770 --> 00:32:51.390
But what do you all think?

00:32:51.390 --> 00:32:53.070
Is productivity culture toxic

00:32:53.070 --> 00:32:55.680
or just a harmless quest
for self improvement?

00:32:55.680 --> 00:32:58.200
And are we all still just
Protestant Work Ethicing

00:32:58.200 --> 00:32:59.250
in ourselves to death?

00:32:59.250 --> 00:33:00.360
Let us know in the comments.

00:33:00.360 --> 00:33:01.350
Be sure to like the video

00:33:01.350 --> 00:33:03.180
to tell us that we
worked hard enough on it.

00:33:03.180 --> 00:33:05.370
Shout out to our patrons
for all their support.

00:33:05.370 --> 00:33:07.500
Do check out our Patreon page
if you have it in a while.

00:33:07.500 --> 00:33:09.690
And as always, thanks for watching.

00:33:09.690 --> 00:33:10.523
Later.

00:33:10.523 --> 00:33:13.176
(upbeat music)

